    "summary": "Fiery post‑Brent set with soaring Bird Song and explosive Crazy Fingers",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Bird Song, an incendiary Crazy Fingers, and a powerhouse Playing In The Band jam. Jerry’s lead work is on fire while Vince Welnick slots in smoothly after Brent’s passing. The Miller soundboard capture is clear enough to convey the emotional tribute.",
    "review": "Richfield Coliseum on September 7‑1990 marked the Dead’s first outing with Vince Welnick at the keyboards, a poignant transition after Brent Mydland’s untimely death. The band rose from a tentative opening – a shaky “Cold Rain And Snow” – into an emotionally charged second half that feels like a collective eulogy. Jerry Garcia was described as \"on fire,\" delivering blistering leads on both Crazy Fingers and the soaring Bird Song, while his solo on Playing In The Band ignites a jam that rockets through Space into a joyous climax. Vince’s synth debut earned praise for fitting the band’s sound and adding high‑energy texture, especially during the “Crazy Fingers” segment. Bob Weir’s rhythm work and vocal harmonies held the groove tight, and the dual drummers provided a solid, propulsive foundation throughout. The set closes with a heartfelt Knockin’ On Heaven’s Door that caps an evening of tribute, triumph, and pure Dead magic.\n\nThe Miller soundboard recording (gd1990-09-07.sbd.miller.92240.sbeok.flac16) captures the performance with good clarity despite minor frequency‑balance quirks, making it the most reliable source for this historic night. Fans looking for the definitive version of the post‑Brent energy should start here.",
